subsection.p:

          You can nest views, but you need to be careful with Scope as it links automatically all components 
          (including SPanels) in the main SPanel with the model and controller associated with the main SPanel. 

          The solution to this issue is to separate the subview from the parent view by using a SUnboundPanel in between. 



          Now you can nest the child MVC cluster into the Top MVC cluster:

public class TopView extends SPanel {
	private SUnboundPanel separatorPanel = new SUnboundPanel();
	// childView is created at the same time as the top view
	private ChildView childView = new ChildView();	
public TopView() {
		this.add(separatorPanel);
		separatorPanel.add(childView);
}
public ChildView getChildView() { return childView; }
}
public class TopController extends BasicController {
	public TopController() {
		TopView view = new TopView();
		setModel(new TopModel());
		setView(view);
		ChildController childController = new ChildController(view.getChildView());
		this.addChild(childController);
	}
}
public class ChildController extends BasicController {
	public ChildController(ChildView view) {
		setModel(new ChildModel());
		setView(view);
	}
}
          And you can benefit from the power of the Hirerchical MVC pattern because that now that you have a hierarchy of controllers,
          the sub controllers don’t have to handle themselves all the controls they receive, and they can delegate some of the controls 
          to their parent controllers.
